## 9. Alternative Concept – Non-Member Friend Functions

The operators can also be implemented as non-member friend functions.

Example:

```cpp
friend Money operator+(const Money& a, const Money& b) {
    return Money(0, a.paise + b.paise);
}
```

Another alternative is using named functions:

```cpp
Money add(Money a, Money b) {
    return Money(0, a.paise + b.paise);
}
```

### Comparison

| Method | Time Complexity | Space Complexity |
|---|---|---|
| Member operator | O(1) | O(1) |
| Friend operator | O(1) | O(1) |
| Named function | O(1) | O(1) |

All three methods have the same time and space complexity.

**Which method allows `100 + m` and `m + 100`?**

A non-member friend operator can support both expressions because the compiler can convert either operand into a `Money` object, provided the constructor allows implicit conversion.

A member operator alone supports `m + 100`, but not `100 + m`, because the left operand is an integer.

## 10. Conclusion

Operator overloading makes money calculations easier to read and understand. The `Money` class stores amounts in paise using `long long`, avoiding floating-point precision errors. Arithmetic, comparison and stream operators were successfully overloaded.
